  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1253 AM CST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nAs of early Tuesday morning, a neutral H5 trough exists across \nthe southern Plains with surface low pressure organizing over \nthe western Great Lakes. Expect the trough to become positively-\ntilted as it progresses eastward, with the surface low moving \nNW. Strong WAA will bring very mild temperatures (highs in the \nmiddle to upper 70s) and elevated dew point temperatures (lower \nto middle 60s) to the region this afternoon. Additionally, \ngradient winds will gust to 25-35 mph at times from the south \ntoday. With plentiful cloud cover, it will be difficult for \nhigher gusts to mix down, but isolated gusts up to 40 mph cannot\nbe ruled out. \n\nSPC has areas near and west of the Mississippi River under a\nMRGL risk for its Day 1 outlook. Convection will develop ahead\nof the surface cold front across the Ozarks this morning and \nafternoon and gradually march to the east. Storm-relative flow \nwill be more SW to NE, so the overall progression of the \nprecipitation will be pretty slow. \n\nLooking the the latest CAMs, it does appear that  there will be\nsome very meager SBCAPE over the MRGL risk area this afternoon,\nbut will quickly dissipate by tonight east of the Mississippi \nRiver. The best shear will actually develop during the evening \nhours after the SBCAPE has dissipated. Cannot rule out a few \nstrong wind gusts mixing down (or even a brief/weak QLCS spin-up\ntornado), but overall the ingredients for significant severe do\nnot appear to line up. Given the SSW- NNE trajectory of the \nconvective elements within the complex of showers, locally \nheavy rain from training thunderstorms will also be possible. \nSome locations could receive a quick 0.75-1.5\" of rain under the\nheaviest activity. \n\nFollowing the passages of the main complex of showers and\nthunderstorms late tonight into early Wednesday morning, a few\nwraparound showers from the surface low over the Great Lakes may\nimpact the EVV Tri-State, followed by a return of dry conditions\nwith clearing skies. Despite the frontal passage, the parent\nsurface low will weaken with zonal mid-level flow returning.\nThis means that temperatures will remain mild (above-normal by\n5-10 degrees) Wednesday into Thursday. A couple dry cold fronts\nwill pass through the region Friday and again Sunday, bringing a\ncool down back to near-normal temperatures for the end of this\nweek into early next week. Temperatures may get cold enough to\nallow for another frost or potentially a freeze, which would\nbring what's left of the growing season to an end.",
